module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.bounded_natural_arithmetic_evaluated_as_unbounded_int

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }

data bounded_natural_arithmetic_evaluated_as_unbounded_int: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "bounded_natural_arithmetic_evaluated_as_unbounded_int" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "bounded natural arithmetic evaluated as unbounded Int (the v1 interpreter evaluates infix + - * / % on std.integer's bounded naturals -- Compose<UInt, MachineWidth<N>>, e.g. UInt8 and UInt32 -- as ordinary 64-bit Int arithmetic, with no check that operands or results lie in [0, 2^N)). INVALID STATE: a value typed UInt32 that is not a 32-bit unsigned natural. HARM: the interpreter answers a program the declared types do not admit, silently, and the emitted realization of the same program disagrees -- std.operator_realization refuses infix arithmetic on a structural operand (the corpus's rule is that structural arithmetic is a call to a declared operation with a typed outcome), so a closure that passes every interpreted witness does not build natively, and nothing in the interpreted run said the arithmetic was outside its type.",

    "RECEIPT, 2026-09-19 (lane A of the approval app, the .dag crypto). UNLANDED NAMES, read as such: std.bitwise, std.bignat, extdeps.crypto.sha2, extdeps.crypto.nist_p256, word32_xor / word32_or / word32_add and test.claim.sha256_fips_witness_test exist only on gunbc#11645 (DRAFT, branch session/swift-ibex-621-dag-crypto, commit cabb3a9) and do NOT resolve in any tree that lacks it; they are provenance for this receipt, not citations of declarations here (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode unlanded_citation_indistinguishable_at_the_citing_end). std.bitwise word32_xor is written `a + b - 2 * word32_and(a: a, b: b)` over a, b: UInt32, so `a + b` reaches 2^33 - 2 while typed UInt32; word32_or, word32_add ((a + b) % 2^32) and the rotations likewise pass through values above 2^32 - 1 typed UInt32. Under the interpreter every FIPS 180-2 SHA-256 vector passed (test.claim.sha256_fips_witness_test, 7/7) and the RFC 6979 A.2.5 P-256 verify answered true, while emitting the same closure produced 18 compile_error! operator refusals on UInt32/UInt8 (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode accepted_source_emits_uncompilable_target, occurrence 2026-09-19). The interpreter was the permissive side; the emitter was right.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS: not an overflow of the 64-bit Int (that refuses, IntegerOverflow); the refusal missing is at the DECLARED width, which the interpreter never consults -- MachineWidth<bits> is a bodiless type and std.machine_constraints Compose is a Phantom, so no evaluation path can see N (measured: a generic fn returning its width parameter N as a value refuses NoSuchVariable). RUNG FOUND AT: below the ladder -- silent wrongness at the declared type. THREE THINGS ARE KEPT APART HERE, because naming a prerequisite as the next rung lets it be satisfied while the invalid path is unchanged. PREREQUISITE: MachineWidth<N> exposes N to .dag as a value (measured absent: a generic fn returning its width parameter N refuses NoSuchVariable), which makes the repair EXPRESSIBLE as one family of operations derived over N instead of a hand-restated width per type, and changes nothing on the path that is wrong. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: the interpreter consumes the operand's declaration for bounded-natural infix arithmetic and either refuses it through the same std.operator_realization operator_realization_for decision the emitter uses (OperatorRealizationRefused), or dispatches it to declared std.integer operations with width admission and a typed overflow outcome (IntegerOverflowSemantics) -- SUFFICIENT FOR the interpreted and emitted realizations of one Accepted program to agree on every bounded-natural arithmetic site, so no interpreted run answers a value outside its declared width. STRUCTURAL CEILING: a sole-constructed bounded-natural carrier whose range admission is its only mint, SUFFICIENT FOR an out-of-width value to be uninhabitable rather than refused.",

    "OCCURRENCE, 2026-09-21 (zesty-raven-800): THE FIRST CONSTRUCTING CONSUMER OF AN UNINHABITED CARRIER. This row already records that Compose is a Phantom and that no evaluation path can see N. What had not happened until gunbc#11643 is that something tried to PUT A VALUE IN ONE. Three declarations in std.integer did: `UInt8Result.UInt8Ready { value: UInt8 }` held an Int in a field typed by an uninhabited carrier, `qualified_octets(members: List<UInt8>)` sealed a list of them, and `uint8_int_value(v: UInt8) -> Int` was `v + 0` -- an identity add whose only purpose was to cross back out of a type that never held anything. The checker admitted all three because the composition is transparent to Int at a formal, which is the SAME permissiveness this row files, reached through construction rather than through arithmetic.",

    "WHAT IT COST, AND WHY IT SURFACED ONLY AT EMISSION: declarations over UInt8 typecheck and their witnesses evaluate under the interpreter, where the transparency holds; their emitted Rust is produced by nothing on the merge path. The Rust emitter renders Compose faithfully as PhantomData, so the first consumers to EMIT the closure refused -- `//gunbc/instruments:self-host` (observed by gunbc#11915 at main 87c6658641e) and `//gunbc/instruments:v2-native-cli` (observed here) -- with the same three errors in the emitted std_integer.rs: one `operator realization: host operator + on structural operand std.integer.UInt8` (the identity add, which is this row's own arithmetic class) and two `E0308 expected PhantomData<(i64, MachineWidth<()>)>, found i64` (the two construction sites). One model-adjacent defect blocked BOTH retained native build subjects, because std.integer is in every closure.",

    "THE CHAIN, AND WHERE IT WAS DECIDED NOT TO CUT. The tempting repair was to declare Compose<A, M> = A and make the carrier transparent in the model too. THAT WAS REJECTED because it contradicts this row: it would make N permanently invisible and cement the defect filed here into std, while the emitter -- the side this row says was RIGHT -- would have been changed to match the permissive side. The repair instead narrows what the three sites CLAIM: they now say what they compute (Int, and List<Int> for the sealed carrier and its accessor), and the identity add is DELETED rather than realized. std.machine_constraints and v1.compiler.emit_rust are untouched.",

    "THE PRECEDENT IS ON MAIN AND IS CITED RATHER THAN RE-ARGUED: std.encoding base64_octets had already made exactly this move one layer up -- `fn base64_octets(values: List<Int>) -> QualifiedOctetsResult` -- and its annotation gives the reason in the same terms, that taking List<UInt8> `MADE IT DECORATIVE` because `UInt8 is a phantom transparent to Int`. So this occurrence is that repair applied to the sites that had not yet taken it.",

    "NO ENFORCED RUNG DROPS, and the reason is this row's own finding: a phantom brand checks nothing, so there was no wall at these three sites to remove. The admission that does work -- uint8_of_int refusing anything outside [0, 2^8) -- is untouched and is still the only way to reach UInt8Ready. WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED: the width is still not stated in any type that can carry it, so this row's STRUCTURAL CEILING is unchanged and remains the trigger -- a sole-constructed bounded-natural carrier whose range admission is its only mint. THE CLIMB IS A NEWTYPE OVER THE CARRIER, NEVER A RETURN TO PHANTOM: an author who correctly concludes that the brand should be enforced must not reach for the row this occurrence is repairing, because a phantom cannot hold the value.",

    "NAMED REMAINDER, 2026-09-21: STD.ENCODING'S DECODE PATH IS STILL SPELLED OVER THE UNINHABITED CARRIER, and it is recorded here rather than left for the next reader to rediscover. The occurrence above repaired the three CONSTRUCTING sites and everything the sealed carrier feeds. It did NOT repair the decode path: `base64_decode_values` and `base64_decode` answer `List<UInt8>?`, `utf8_decode_octets` takes `List<UInt8>`, `utf8_admit_octet` takes `b: UInt8` and hands it to `word_of_int`'s `Int` formal, and `extdeps.npm` `npm_sri_base64_payload_is_canonical` and `extdeps.mercurial` `mercurial_node_id_from_octets` (with its twenty-plus `octetNN: UInt8` fields) sit behind them.",

    "WHY IT WAS LEFT, AND THE REASON IS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A BUDGET. Retyping the two utf8 declarations alone RELOCATES the defect instead of removing it: `base64_decode` feeds `utf8_decode_octets`, so today BOTH sides of that call spell `UInt8` and the boundary typechecks under emission -- PhantomData meets PhantomData -- while the mismatch sits INSIDE `utf8_admit_octet`. Retyped, a `List<i64>` formal would face a `List<PhantomData>` argument at a call that currently has no mismatch at all. One emission defect before, one after, in a different place. Removing it requires retyping the producer and the two public surfaces behind it, which is a different decision with different consumers.",

    "AND IT IS NOT A REGRESSION, which is what makes deferring it honest rather than convenient. This path did not emit cleanly BEFORE the repair either: the crossing that was deleted (`uint8_int_value`, an identity add over the uninhabited carrier) rendered as `compile_error!` -- \"operator realization: host operator `+` on structural operand std.integer.UInt8\" -- which is this row's own arithmetic class. So the path has been unemittable throughout and the repair exchanged one unemittable form for another at a site it was obliged to touch. Nothing that worked stopped working.",

    "TRIGGER FOR THIS REMAINDER, AT CAPABILITY GRAIN: A RETAINED CLOSURE EMITS std.encoding'S DECODE PATH (`base64_decode` -> `utf8_decode_octets`). It is latent for exactly one reason -- neither retained native subject's closure reaches that path -- so the moment one does, the emission fails and the question becomes live. The retype-versus-newtype decision for `base64_decode`, `extdeps.npm` and `extdeps.mercurial` is MADE THEN AND NOT BEFORE, on the evidence that closure produces. Deliberately NOT written as \"retype base64_decode\": that names one of the two answers, and an artifact-grain trigger would be satisfied by a retype that the newtype climb should have taken instead.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.machine_constraints", decl_name: "MachineWidth", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.integer", decl_name: "UInt32", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.integer", decl_name: "IntegerOverflowSemantics", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.operator_realization", decl_name: "operator_realization_for", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.operator_realization", decl_name: "operator_realization_refusal_message", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
